window.SUPER_BANK.push(...[{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:d10-15","chapter":10,"mode":"delegation","topic":"Delegation safety & five rights","prompt":"A nurse has not confirmed that you are trained and competent to perform a task before asking you to do it. Which right is most directly in question?","choices":["Right Person.","Right Circumstances.","Right Directions and Communication.","Right Supervision and Evaluation."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The Right Person asks whether this particular worker is trained, competent and authorized to perform the task.","source":"Teamwork slides, “Five Rights of Delegation/Assigning”; Complete Chapter 10 Quiz Study Guide","originalId":"d10-15","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:d10-16","chapter":10,"mode":"delegation","topic":"Delegation safety & five rights","prompt":"A nurse asks you to perform a task that employer policy prohibits. What is the safest response?","choices":["Do not accept it; explain the policy concern and notify or clarify with the supervisor.","Accept because a nurse’s request overrides employer policy.","Ask the client to approve an exception.","Ask another support worker to perform it."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A task must be permitted by employer policy and the worker’s job description, as well as applicable requirements. A supervisor’s request does not override those limits.","source":"Chapter 10, Box 10.3 and “The Support Worker’s Role in Delegation”; Complete Chapter 10 Quiz Study Guide","originalId":"d10-16","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:s10-09","chapter":10,"mode":"supervision","topic":"Supervision & communication","prompt":"You have tried to resolve a routine coworker conflict privately and respectfully, but it remains unresolved. What is an appropriate next step?","choices":["Seek help through the supervisor or another suitable third party, such as HR or a union representative.","Repeat the same private discussion indefinitely before involving anyone else.","Ask uninvolved colleagues to choose which worker’s account is correct.","Limit necessary handoff information until the coworker agrees to resolve the dispute."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The notes recommend escalation through a supervisor, HR, union representative or suitable third party when a private, respectful attempt does not resolve the conflict.","source":"Chapter 10, Box 10.2; Complete Chapter 10 Quiz Study Guide","originalId":"s10-09","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:s10-10","chapter":10,"mode":"supervision","topic":"Supervision & communication","prompt":"A client’s family offers extra pay for a clinical task that is outside your authorized role. What does the offer change?","choices":["It does not change your legal authority or role limits; refer the request to your supervisor.","It authorizes the task if the client agrees.","It allows the family to supervise you.","It turns the task into an ordinary private-contract duty."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Extra pay, client requests or family requests do not override role limits or applicable restrictions. Raise the request with your supervisor and follow policy.","source":"Complete Chapter 10 Quiz Study Guide; Chapter 10, “Working Under Supervision” and “Assigning and Delegating”","originalId":"s10-10","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:p13","mode":"principles","topic":"Four principles","prompt":"A worker performs a risky procedure without training or authorization. Which principle is violated when this puts the client at risk of harm?","choices":["Nonmaleficence","Beneficence","Justice","Autonomy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Nonmaleficence means seeking to do no harm. When a question asks which principle is violated, focus on the risk created by acting outside competence or authorization.","source":"Chapter 4 Ethics PowerPoint, “Nonmaleficence”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"p13","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:t10-10","chapter":10,"mode":"teamwork","topic":"Teamwork & collaboration","prompt":"A team includes nurses, support workers, therapists and social workers who coordinate care from their different professional perspectives. Which description best fits this team?","choices":["An interdisciplinary or multidisciplinary health-care team.","A private employment contract.","A delegation directive.","A family conference with no care providers."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The post-quiz lecture treated multidisciplinary and interprofessional teams as equivalent descriptions of providers working together across disciplines. Team members communicate observations and coordinate care.","source":"Chapter 10, “The Health Care Team”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"t10-10","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:t10-11","chapter":10,"mode":"delegation","topic":"Delegation safety & five rights","prompt":"A nurse tells you that a previously delegated task is withdrawn. What should you do?","choices":["Stop performing that delegated task and clarify any next steps with the nurse.","Continue because you were trained earlier.","Ask another support worker to take over the delegation.","Perform it only when the nurse is off-site."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The nurse can withdraw a delegation. Stop performing the task when it is withdrawn; do not transfer the authorization to another support worker.","source":"Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review; Chapter 10, “The Support Worker’s Role in Delegation”","originalId":"t10-11","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:t10-12","chapter":10,"mode":"supervision","topic":"Supervision & communication","prompt":"A resident who was alert earlier becomes suddenly confused and unsteady during your visit. When should you report this change to the nurse?","choices":["At once, using the established contact procedure.","After waiting 20 minutes to see whether it passes.","At the end of your shift.","Only if the resident falls."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Report a change in the client’s condition to the nurse promptly. Do not delay a safety concern while waiting to see what happens.","source":"Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review; Chapter 10, “Accepting a Task”","originalId":"t10-12","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:t10-13","chapter":10,"mode":"delegation","topic":"Delegation safety & five rights","prompt":"You realize that you missed a scheduled care task. What is the accountable response?","choices":["Tell the supervisor promptly, give factual details and follow direction for continuity of care.","Hide the omission if the client seems comfortable.","Record that the task was completed.","Ask a coworker to document it as theirs."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Accountability includes explaining actions and omissions. Report missed care promptly and honestly so the team can address the client’s needs; never document care that was not provided.","source":"Chapter 10, Key Terms, “Accountability”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"t10-13","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:d10-17","chapter":10,"mode":"delegation","topic":"Delegation safety & five rights","prompt":"Which option is NOT one of the five rights of delegation?","choices":["Right Outcome","Right Task","Right Person","Right Supervision and Evaluation"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The five rights are Right Task, Right Circumstances, Right Person, Right Directions and Communication, and Right Supervision and Evaluation. “Right Outcome” is not one of the five.","source":"Teamwork slides, “Five Rights of Delegation/Assigning”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"d10-17","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:s10-11","chapter":10,"mode":"supervision","topic":"Supervision & communication","prompt":"You are working alone in home support and need guidance for a delegated task. Which step best supports safe supervision?","choices":["Use the designated phone or after-hours contact and confirm what to do before proceeding if support or direction is unclear.","Assume off-site supervision is unnecessary because the nurse is not in the home.","Ask a family member to replace the nurse.","Continue without checking because you have completed similar tasks."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Supervision may be on-site or by telephone. Know the correct supervisor, contact numbers and backup procedures; working alone does not remove the need for appropriate direction and support.","source":"Chapter 10, “Working Under Supervision”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"s10-11","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:r13","mode":"rights","topic":"Client rights & consent","prompt":"A client is reviewing information before planned care. Which item does NOT belong among the elements of informed consent?","choices":["The worker’s personal opinion about the client’s choice.","The reason for the care.","The risks or side effects and available alternatives.","The consequences of declining."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The elements cover why, what, how, who, expected outcomes, risks or side effects, alternatives and consequences of declining. A worker’s personal opinion is not an element. Support workers do not provide specialized medical explanations or obtain written medical consent.","source":"Legislation slides, “Informed Consent”; Chapter 5, “Informed Consent”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"r13","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:r14","mode":"rights","topic":"Client rights & consent","prompt":"A resident wants to display family photographs and keep appropriate personal furnishings in their room. What right is most directly involved?","choices":["The right to keep and display personal possessions and make the room feel like home, subject to legitimate facility restrictions.","The right to make staffing decisions for the whole facility.","The right to restrict other residents’ family visits.","The right to change another resident’s care plan."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Residents’ rights include keeping and displaying appropriate personal belongings and making their room feel like home, subject to legitimate facility restrictions.","source":"Legislation slides, “Residents’ Bill of Rights”; Chapter 5; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"r14","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:r15","mode":"rights","topic":"Client rights & consent","prompt":"A resident is left without suitable clothing and staff do not assist with basic grooming. Which residents’ right is most directly affected?","choices":["The right to be properly sheltered, fed, clothed and groomed.","The right to appoint a family member as a staff supervisor.","The right to choose which legislation applies.","The right to avoid all contact with health-care providers."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The Residents’ Bill of Rights includes being properly sheltered, fed, clothed and groomed. Report unmet basic needs through the appropriate care channel.","source":"Legislation slides, “Residents’ Bill of Rights”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"r15","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:r16","mode":"rights","topic":"Client rights & consent","prompt":"A worker tells a resident that family visits are banned simply because the visits make the worker’s shift less convenient. What is the best assessment?","choices":["The worker is improperly interfering with the resident’s contact with family.","The worker may set visit rules based on personal convenience.","The resident has lost privacy rights by living in a facility.","The family can take over the care plan without the resident."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A support worker cannot arbitrarily restrict family contact. Follow applicable facility procedures and raise legitimate safety or legal restrictions through the supervisor.","source":"Legislation slides, “Residents’ Bill of Rights”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"r16","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:r17","mode":"rights","topic":"Client rights & consent","prompt":"A resident is protected from emotional, physical, sexual and financial abuse. Which right does this describe?","choices":["The right to be free from abuse.","The right to control staff employment.","The right to refuse all documentation.","The right to make decisions for other residents."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The course review identifies freedom from emotional, physical, sexual and financial abuse as a residents’ right. Report suspected abuse through required workplace channels.","source":"Legislation slides, “Residents’ Bill of Rights”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"r17","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:r18","mode":"rights","topic":"Client rights & consent","prompt":"A support worker sees a “DNR” tattoo on a client. What is the safest understanding?","choices":["A tattoo alone is not a substitute for applicable medical orders or documentation; follow workplace procedures and confirm with the nurse.","The tattoo always authorizes the worker to withhold any care.","The worker should remove the tattoo from the client record.","The worker can determine the order’s legal effect independently."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Do not infer consent or a medical order from a tattoo. Follow the applicable documented orders and clarify with the regulated professional; do not make an independent legal or clinical determination.","source":"Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"r18","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:r19","mode":"rights","topic":"Client rights & consent","prompt":"A will names an executrix. What is her role?","choices":["To carry out the estate responsibilities assigned by the will.","To make routine health-care decisions for every resident.","To issue medical orders before the client’s death.","To supervise support workers in the facility."],"answer":[0],"explain":"An executor or executrix is appointed to carry out responsibilities under a will. An estate role does not by itself make someone a health-care decision-maker for a living client.","source":"Chapter 5, “Wills”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"r19","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:v07","mode":"privacy","topic":"Privacy & confidentiality","prompt":"A nurse who is not involved in a client’s care asks you to share the client’s diagnosis out of curiosity. What should you do?","choices":["Do not disclose it; share client information only for an authorized care purpose with someone who needs it.","Share it because the person is a nurse.","Give only the diagnosis and omit the client’s name.","Discuss it in a public area so the client cannot hear."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Staff membership alone does not create a need to know. Protect confidential information and share only with authorized people for a care-related purpose through an appropriate channel.","source":"Chapter 5, “The Right to Privacy and Confidentiality”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"v07","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:t09","mode":"torts","topic":"Legal issues & torts","prompt":"Which option is NOT an intentional tort?","choices":["Negligence","Assault","Battery","False imprisonment"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Negligence is an unintentional tort involving failure to use reasonable care that results in harm. Assault, battery and false imprisonment are intentional torts.","source":"Chapter 5, “Torts”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"t09","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:t10","mode":"torts","topic":"Legal issues & torts","prompt":"A worker writes and circulates a harmful false statement about a client. Which form of defamation is this?","choices":["Libel","Slander","Assault","Battery"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Libel is written or pictorial defamation. Slander is spoken defamation.","source":"Chapter 5, “Defamation”; Legislation slides, “Defamation”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"t10","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:t11","mode":"torts","topic":"Legal issues & torts","prompt":"Staff take away a resident’s walker solely to prevent the resident from leaving, without an appropriate basis to restrict movement. Which tort could this illustrate?","choices":["False imprisonment","Slander","Negligence only","Assault"],"answer":[0],"explain":"False imprisonment can involve unlawfully restricting a person’s freedom of movement. The specific circumstances and applicable authorization matter; do not assume every mobility aid restriction has the same legal meaning.","source":"Chapter 5, “False Imprisonment”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"t11","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:w09","mode":"workers","topic":"Worker rights & safe practice","prompt":"What are two central purposes of human rights legislation?","choices":["Prevent discrimination and promote or advance human rights.","Set wages and award workers’ compensation.","Create criminal offences and regulate estate distribution.","Assign care tasks and delegate controlled acts."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Human rights legislation aims to prevent discrimination and promote or advance human rights.","source":"Legislation slides, “Human Rights Legislation”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"w09","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:w10","mode":"workers","topic":"Worker rights & safe practice","prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the Canadian Human Rights Act from the BC Human Rights Code?","choices":["The Canadian Human Rights Act is federal; the BC Human Rights Code is provincial.","Both names refer to one federal law.","The BC Human Rights Code is federal; the Canadian Human Rights Act is municipal.","Neither applies to discrimination."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The Canadian Human Rights Act is federal legislation, while the BC Human Rights Code is provincial. Apply the law relevant to the setting and situation.","source":"Legislation slides, “Human Rights Legislation”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"w10","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-01","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A capable client declines a bath before a family visit. The family says the client will be embarrassed and asks you to proceed anyway. Which response best respects the client while addressing the concern?","choices":["Ask the client privately what they prefer, offer acceptable alternatives, and report the refusal and outcome according to policy.","Proceed because the family’s concern is about the client’s welfare.","Tell the client that refusing is unfair to the family, then ask again.","Leave without discussing alternatives because autonomy rules out further conversation."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Autonomy protects the client’s choice, including refusal. Beneficence supports exploring a reasonable alternative, while professional practice requires reporting the refusal and outcome through the approved care channel.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Autonomy,” PDF p. 6; Chapter 4, “Autonomy”; Ethics Consolidated Arcade Notes","originalId":"x4-01","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-02","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A client refuses a mobility aid. You believe the choice increases fall risk, but you have no new observation of immediate danger. What is the best first response?","choices":["Respect the decision, ask about the client’s reasons, share the concern with the supervisor, and follow the care plan.","Hide the aid so the client cannot refuse it.","Tell the client the refusal violates the care plan and insist.","Ask the family to make the decision instead."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Autonomy supports the client’s decision. Beneficence and nonmaleficence make it appropriate to raise a genuine safety concern, but the support worker should not coerce the client or transfer the decision to family without authority.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Autonomy,” PDF p. 6; Chapter 4, “Autonomy” and “Nonmaleficence”","originalId":"x4-02","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-03","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A client with a challenging temperament receives shorter visits than other clients even though the care plan and needs are similar. Which principle is being violated most directly?","choices":["Justice, because the client is receiving less fair and equal treatment based on temperament.","Autonomy, because every client must receive identical tasks.","Beneficence, because the worker must always do extra tasks.","Nonmaleficence, because any difference in visit length causes harm."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Justice requires fair and equal treatment. Fairness does not require identical care when needs differ, but a client’s temperament is not a sound reason to reduce appropriate care.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Justice,” PDF p. 7; Chapter 4, “Justice”; Ethics Consolidated Arcade Notes","originalId":"x4-03","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-04","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A family asks you to skip the client’s planned meal support because they believe the client should lose weight. The client has not agreed to this change. What should you do?","choices":["Follow the care plan, avoid changing care based on the family request, and refer the concern to the supervisor.","Skip the meal because the family knows the client’s history.","Tell the client the family is trying to harm them.","Change the care plan yourself because beneficence means choosing what seems healthiest."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Beneficence places the client’s needs at the centre, but the support worker should not independently change the care plan or make clinical judgments. Raise the conflict with the supervisor.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Beneficence,” PDF p. 8; Chapter 4, “Beneficence”","originalId":"x4-04","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-05","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A family asks whether a change in the client’s appetite means the treatment is failing. You observed that the client ate little breakfast. What is the best response?","choices":["Report the factual observation to the appropriate care team member and refer the family’s interpretation question to the nurse.","Explain that the treatment is probably causing appetite loss.","Share your guess if you clearly label it as an opinion.","Refuse to report the meal observation because it is health information."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The worker may report authorized firsthand observations but should not diagnose or interpret medical meaning. This protects the client from inaccurate information and helps the nurse assess the concern.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Nonmaleficence,” PDF pp. 9–10; Ethics Consolidated Arcade Notes, “Nonmaleficence, scope and communication”","originalId":"x4-05","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-06","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A worker has heard only the family’s account of a care conflict and is about to choose an option. Which next step best follows the ethical-dilemma process?","choices":["Gather more relevant information, including the client’s wishes where possible, before comparing options.","Choose the family’s preferred option because it was reported first.","Select the option with the greatest short-term convenience.","Apply nonmaleficence alone without considering the other principles."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The explicit process is to consider the four principles, collect as much information as possible, then consider possible options. Fact-finding comes before choosing.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Dealing with Ethical Dilemmas,” PDF p. 11; Ethics Consolidated Arcade Notes","originalId":"x4-06","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-07","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"Two options both respect the client’s stated preference. One gives a short-term benefit but may create a longer-term risk; the other avoids that risk but may limit an activity the client values. Which evaluation is most complete?","choices":["Compare wishes, fairness, short- and long-term benefit, potential harm, and consequences for the client, family and others.","Choose the option that avoids all risk, without considering the client’s preference.","Choose the option the family finds easiest to support.","Choose whichever option is easiest to document."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The evaluation questions consider autonomy, justice, short- or long-term beneficence, nonmaleficence and the possible consequences for everyone affected. No single principle automatically replaces the full review.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Questions to Ask Yourself…,” PDF pp. 12–13; Ethics Consolidated Arcade Notes","originalId":"x4-07","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x4-08","chapter":4,"mode":"challenge4","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A client offers you money to complete a personal favour that is not part of your role. The client says accepting would make them feel cared for. What is the best response?","choices":["Maintain the professional boundary, explain that the favour is outside your role, and ask the supervisor how to handle the offer under policy.","Accept because refusing could hurt the client’s feelings.","Accept only if you do not tell the employer.","Ask the client to describe the favour before deciding whether to keep it private."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Compassion does not remove professional boundaries. The worker should not take on an unauthorized favour or create a private arrangement; follow employer policy and seek supervisory guidance.","source":"Ethics PowerPoint, “Code of Ethics,” PDF p. 4; Ethics Consolidated Arcade Notes, “Codes of ethics and professional boundaries”","originalId":"x4-08","group":"Chapter 4","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-01","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A client agrees to a bath, then uses a reliable gesture from the care plan to signal “stop” partway through. The client has aphasia. What should you do?","choices":["Stop the care, acknowledge the gesture, preserve privacy, and report the refusal according to policy.","Continue because the client agreed before the bath began.","Assume aphasia means the client cannot refuse.","Ask the family to decide whether the gesture counts."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Consent can be withdrawn during routine care. Aphasia or difficulty speaking does not by itself remove the ability to communicate a choice; follow known communication supports and do not force care.","source":"Legislation slides, “Informed Consent”; Chapter 5, “Informed Consent”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes, “Privacy, confidentiality and consent”","originalId":"x5-01","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-02","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A client asks you to explain the risks, alternatives and likely consequences of refusing a planned surgery. Which response stays within your role?","choices":["Acknowledge the questions and arrange for the nurse or appropriate clinician to explain those medical details before the client decides.","Explain the risks using what you remember from another client.","Tell the client to sign because the surgery is already scheduled.","Ask the family to choose if the client is uncertain."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Risks, alternatives and consequences of refusing are elements of informed consent, but the support worker does not supply specialized medical information or obtain written medical consent. Refer the questions to the appropriate clinician.","source":"Legislation slides, “Informed Consent,” Slide 18; Chapter 5, “Informed Consent”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"x5-02","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-03","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A nurse from another unit asks for a resident’s diagnosis but is not involved in the resident’s care. What is the best response?","choices":["Do not share it based only on the person’s job title; use the approved process to confirm an authorized care-related need.","Share it because every nurse has a general need to know.","Share the diagnosis but not the resident’s name.","Ask the resident’s family whether the nurse seems trustworthy."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Confidentiality depends on an authorized care purpose and need to know, not simply professional title. Use workplace policy to clarify before sharing.","source":"Chapter 5, “The Right to Privacy and Confidentiality”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes, “Privacy, confidentiality and consent”","originalId":"x5-03","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-04","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A resident is injured after being left on the toilet without a call bell, despite the worker knowing the resident may need help. Which legal concept best fits the alleged failure?","choices":["Negligence, an unintentional tort involving failure to use reasonable care that results in harm.","Assault, because the worker did not provide assistance.","Battery, because the resident was seated on the toilet.","Slander, because the call bell was missing."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Negligence concerns a failure to use reasonable care that results in harm. The scenario includes both an omitted safety measure and an injury; the other torts require different facts.","source":"Chapter 5, “Negligence”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes, “Civil/criminal law and torts”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"x5-04","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-05","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A worker threatens to tie down a resident, then actually restrains the resident without appropriate authorization. Which distinction is most accurate?","choices":["The threat may be assault; the actual non-consensual touching may be battery, and the restriction may also raise false-imprisonment concerns.","The threat is battery, while actual restraint is only slander.","Both actions are negligence because no injury is stated.","Neither is legally relevant unless the resident is physically injured."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Assault concerns a threat or attempt that causes fear of unwanted contact; battery concerns actual non-consensual touching. Unlawfully restricting movement may also raise false-imprisonment concerns. Assess the facts and applicable authorization rather than assuming injury is required.","source":"Chapter 5, “Assault and Battery” and “False Imprisonment”; Legislation slides, “Torts”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"x5-05","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-06","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A resident’s walker is removed so the resident cannot leave the unit. Staff say they were “just keeping the resident safe,” but no applicable care direction or authorization is identified. What is the most careful assessment?","choices":["The restriction could raise false-imprisonment concerns; seek the nurse’s direction and follow the applicable care and safety procedures.","It is automatically permissible whenever staff use the word “safety.”","It is libel because staff discussed the walker.","It is always battery because the walker was touched."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Removing a mobility aid solely to restrict movement can illustrate false imprisonment, but the legal assessment depends on authorization and circumstances. Do not assume all mobility restrictions have the same legal effect.","source":"Chapter 5, “False Imprisonment”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes, “Civil/criminal law and torts”","originalId":"x5-06","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-07","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A worker tells a visitor a harmful false statement about a resident, then posts the same statement in writing. Which classification is correct?","choices":["The spoken statement may be slander; the written statement may be libel.","The spoken statement is libel; the written statement is slander.","Both are negligence because they may cause distress.","Both are invasion of privacy only; defamation does not apply."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Slander is spoken defamation; libel is written or pictorial defamation. The statement must be false and harmful to reputation for the distinction to be relevant.","source":"Chapter 5, “Defamation”; Legislation slides, “Defamation,” Slide 32; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes","originalId":"x5-07","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x5-08","chapter":5,"mode":"challenge5","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A resident asks to keep family photographs and a small personal furnishing. A staff member objects because the room “looks untidy,” but identifies no care or safety restriction. What is the best response?","choices":["Respect the resident’s interest in personal possessions and a homelike room, and check legitimate facility restrictions if a specific concern exists.","Remove the items because staff decide how residents’ rooms should look.","Tell the family to seek permission from other residents.","Treat the request as a refusal of care."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Residents may keep appropriate belongings and make their room feel like home, subject to legitimate facility restrictions. Staff preference alone is not a reason to remove personal items.","source":"Legislation slides, “Residents’ Bill of Rights”; Chapter 5 Arcade Update Lecture Notes, “Residents’ rights”","originalId":"x5-08","group":"Chapter 5","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-01","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A task is allowed by law and appears in the worker’s job description, but employer policy prohibits support workers from doing it. The nurse is willing to delegate it. What should the worker do?","choices":["Do not accept it under the current policy; explain the conflict and ask the supervisor to clarify.","Accept because the nurse’s decision overrides employer policy.","Ask the client to approve an exception.","Ask a coworker who has done it before to supervise."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A task must meet applicable legal and professional requirements and be permitted by employer policy and the job description. A delegation request does not erase those limits.","source":"Chapter 10, Box 10.3, p. 157; Chapter 10, “The Delegation Process”; Complete Chapter 10 Quiz Study Guide","originalId":"x10-01","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-02","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A delegated task was appropriate yesterday. Today the client’s condition has changed, and the outcome is no longer predictable. What should you do first?","choices":["Pause the task, contact the nurse at once, and report the change before proceeding.","Continue because the task was delegated previously.","Wait 20 minutes to determine whether the client returns to baseline.","Ask a family member whether the task should continue."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Delegation depends on the client’s current condition and circumstances. A significant change requires prompt reporting and nursing direction; earlier authorization does not make the task automatically appropriate today.","source":"Chapter 10, Boxes 10.4–10.5, pp. 157–158; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"x10-02","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-03","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A nurse gives clear written directions for a delegated task, but no one is available to help if the client’s condition changes. Which right is most directly unmet?","choices":["Right Supervision and Evaluation.","Right Directions and Communication, because written directions can never be used.","Right Person, because off-site work excludes all support workers.","Right Task, because every delegated task must be performed by a nurse."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Clear directions do not replace adequate supervision and timely help. The right supervision and evaluation asks whether the nurse can appropriately oversee performance and respond to problems.","source":"Teamwork slides, “Five Rights of Delegation/Assigning,” Slide 23; Chapter 10, Box 10.4, p. 158","originalId":"x10-03","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-04","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"You realize that a delegated medication-related task may have been missed. You are unsure whether another dose is due. What is the safest response?","choices":["Report the omission promptly and ask the nurse for direction; do not independently give an extra dose or alter the record.","Give another dose to make up for the missed one.","Record the dose as given because it was scheduled.","Wait until the next routine check-in so you can report several issues together."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Support workers are accountable for their actions and omissions. Report medication-related safety concerns promptly, follow authorized responsibilities, and do not independently administer or change a medication task.","source":"Chapter 10, “Accountability” and “Accepting a Task”; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"x10-04","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-05","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A team is unsure who should arrange and coordinate services after a client returns home. Who usually coordinates the community team’s services and monitors the client’s needs?","choices":["The case manager or team leader.","The support worker who has the most daily visits.","The client’s executor or executrix.","The charge nurse from the hospital, in every case."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The case manager or team leader coordinates community services and assesses, monitors and evaluates the client’s needs. The support worker contributes observations but does not take over case management.","source":"Chapter 10, “Teamwork in Community Settings,” p. 153; Teamwork slides, “Teamwork in Community Settings”","originalId":"x10-05","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-06","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A client directly hires you and offers a written contract, extra pay and training for a task outside your safe competence and applicable restrictions. What do those arrangements authorize?","choices":["They do not authorize the task; decline it, explain the concern and seek appropriate supervisory or professional guidance.","They authorize it because the client is the employer.","The training authorizes it even when restrictions prohibit it.","The contract transfers all responsibility from the worker to the client."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A private agreement may clarify duties and work arrangements, but cannot make an unsafe or prohibited task appropriate. Training, pay and a client request do not automatically expand authority or remove individual accountability.","source":"Chapter 10, “Working Under Supervision” and “Assigning and Delegating”; Complete Chapter 10 Quiz Study Guide","originalId":"x10-06","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-07","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"The nurse delegates a procedure to you for one client. A coworker offers to perform it for that client while you take another assignment. What is the safest response?","choices":["Do not transfer or re-delegate the authorization; contact the nurse to arrange a safe assignment.","Let the coworker do it if they have watched you before.","Give the coworker your written directions and sign the record later.","Ask the client to authorize the coworker."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Support workers cannot delegate or pass along a delegated task. Authorization is specific to the worker and client; contact the delegating professional when staffing or workload makes the assignment unsafe.","source":"Chapter 10, “The Support Worker’s Role in Delegation”; Chapter 10 Quiz Study Guide","originalId":"x10-07","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-4-5/data.js:x10-08","chapter":10,"mode":"challenge10","topic":"Integrated challenge scenarios","prompt":"A worker has made a mistake in care but believes reporting it will upset the client. Which response best reflects accountability and teamwork?","choices":["Report the facts promptly through the appropriate channel and follow instructions to protect continuity of care.","Wait to see whether the client notices.","Alter the documentation to avoid alarming the team.","Discuss the mistake only with a coworker who was not involved."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Accountability includes accepting responsibility and explaining actions or omissions. Prompt, factual reporting allows the team to respond to the client’s needs; do not falsify or conceal records.","source":"Chapter 10, Key Terms, “Accountability,” p. 151; Post-Chapter 5 Quiz Review","originalId":"x10-08","group":"Chapter 10","bank":"Chapters 4, 5 & 10","sourceFiles":["chapter-4-5/data.js","chapter-4-5/chapter4_focus.js","chapter-4-5/chapter10.js","chapter-4-5/post_ch5_review.js","chapter-4-5/challenge_rounds.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-001","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Critical reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA repeatedly reviews a tense interaction and becomes more certain that the client was unreasonable. Which addition would most clearly turn this review into productive reflection?","choices":["Examine personal assumptions, compare intentions with effects, and plan a change to evaluate.","Reconstruct the conversation until the HCA can prove the original approach was justified.","Identify the client’s personality type to predict future refusals.","Focus on feelings without considering subsequent actions."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Reflection examines the self and contributing factors and uses insight to guide future practice. Rehearsing blame, proving oneself right, or stopping at emotion does not complete that learning process.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-001","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-002","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Reflective versus reflexive","difficulty":2,"prompt":"During care, an HCA notices that fear of criticism is making their tone abrupt. They slow down, clarify the client’s concern, and later examine the interaction in writing. Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":["Changing tone during care makes later reflection unnecessary.","Only the later writing is relevant to self-awareness.","The HCA is reflecting the client’s feelings back, so this is exclusively therapeutic reflection.","The in-the-moment examination illustrates reflexivity, complemented by later reflection."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The chapter highlights simultaneously examining oneself and interacting as an aspect of reflexivity. Later reflection can consolidate learning. Reflecting a client’s feelings back is a different communication process.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-002","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-003","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Self-reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA says, “You seem disappointed that the visit ended early.” What additional activity would specifically demonstrate self-reflection?","choices":["Asking a colleague to repeat the statement.","Observing whether the client nods.","Repeating the statement in simpler language.","Considering whether the HCA’s own discomfort with sadness led them to end the visit abruptly."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Reflecting perceived feelings back to a client differs from examining one’s own motives, reactions, and contribution.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-003","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-004","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Critical reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"After an interaction, an HCA accurately lists everything said but concludes, “There is nothing else to examine because the facts are recorded.” What is the main limitation?","choices":["Reflection must exclude description to avoid repetition.","Only unsuccessful interactions require analysis.","A factual description must be replaced by emotional impressions.","The account omits analysis of meaning, assumptions, contributing factors, and future responses."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Description is a foundation, not the whole reflective process. Analysis and resulting learning give the account practical value.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-004","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-005","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Outcomes","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client accepts assistance and the task is completed, but the HCA remembers interrupting them several times. Which reflective conclusion is strongest?","choices":["The interruptions invalidate every positive aspect of the encounter.","Evaluate both the overall outcome and individual events; retain helpful actions while examining avoidable interruptions.","Completion proves the interruptions were necessary.","Only the client’s final words should influence future practice."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The chapter considers the overall result while also exploring details and reactions. A positive endpoint does not remove the need to examine potentially harmful communication.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-005","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-006","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Seven-step cycle","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA has described a recurring interaction, explored reactions and contributing factors, and identified a need to improve. What should follow before choosing specific strategies?","choices":["Establish a goal for future similar interactions.","Repeat the description until no emotion remains.","Apply the first available technique.","Classify the client’s defence mechanism."],"answer":[0],"explain":"In the seven-step reflective sequence, analysis informs future goals; strategies and action plans follow those goals.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-006","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-007","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Seven-step cycle","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which sequence best represents the seven-step reflective cycle used in this course?","choices":["Allocate time; describe; diagnose others; choose a goal; suppress feelings; act; finish permanently.","Describe; choose an experience; act; explore reactions; schedule time; plan; set goals.","Allocate time; choose an experience; describe and explore it; examine reactions and results; establish goals; develop a plan; apply learning in practice.","Choose an experience; set goals; allocate time; act; describe; plan; examine reactions."],"answer":[2],"explain":"This preserves the course sequence: time, experience, description, exploration of reactions/results, goals, plan, and implementation. Further experience feeds the cycle. Labels are paraphrased rather than presented as verbatim diagram text.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-007","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-008","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Goals and plans","difficulty":2,"prompt":"“At my next three morning-care encounters, I will ask the client’s preferred order before beginning and review whether this improved participation.” Which distinction is most accurate?","choices":["Asking about order is a goal; improved participation is a description.","Reviewing encounters replaces the need to apply the strategy.","Improved participation is the intended outcome; asking about order is a strategy; reviewing encounters supports evaluation.","The statement concerns only the client and therefore cannot be reflective."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The outcome sought, the action intended, and the later evaluation serve different functions. This makes learning actionable rather than merely aspirational.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-008","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-009","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Johns model","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA writes, “I could have paused, but I feared appearing incompetent. A pause might have allowed the client to explain.” Which Johns-model element is most directly developed?","choices":["Resultant learning only.","Description of the experience.","Alternative actions, why they were not chosen, and their possible consequences.","Significant background information only."],"answer":[2],"explain":"This considers an alternative, the barrier to selecting it, and its likely consequences. Several elements can overlap, but this is the explicit focus.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-009","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-010","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Johns model","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which reflection most directly examines the aims of an action, rather than its consequences?","choices":["The client stopped speaking after I interrupted.","The corridor was noisy and the client had slept poorly.","I interrupted because I wanted to finish quickly and reassure myself that I was efficient.","Next time I will allow a longer pause."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Aims concern what the worker was trying to achieve and reasons for acting. Silence is a consequence, noise is background, and a future pause is learning or planning.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-010","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-011","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Johns model","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A written reflection includes events, possible causes, background, aims, consequences, and alternative actions. It ends without specifying what was learned. What is missing from Johns’s seven-part examination?","choices":["A diagnosis explaining the HCA’s reaction.","An assurance that the same technique will work for everyone.","Proof that the client’s emotions were irrational.","Resultant learning and how future reactions may change."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Johns’s final element connects the examination to learning and future reactions. The model seeks depth, not completion of a checklist alone.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-011","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-012","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Intentions and context","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A daughter initially offers to care for her mother at home. Shortly before a planning meeting, she discovers an unexpected care need and becomes hesitant. What is the strongest initial interpretation?","choices":["Her siblings should decide before she becomes more emotional.","The new event may have changed her feelings, expectations, or perceived ability to help; clarify these rather than assuming motives.","Her earlier offer was dishonest.","Her hesitation demonstrates that home care is unsuitable."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The chapter’s family-meeting example highlights how an unexpected event can alter emotions and intentions. The explanation remains a hypothesis to explore, not an established motive or placement decision.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-012","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-013","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Emotional needs","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA becomes resentful when clients do not thank them. Which response best addresses the underlying professional-boundary concern?","choices":["Conclude that caring requires having no emotional needs.","Recognize the need for appreciation, seek appropriate support outside client relationships, and examine its effect on care.","Provide more help to clients who offer praise.","Ask each client to acknowledge the HCA’s effort."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The chapter warns against centring care on fulfilment of the worker’s dominant personal needs. Needs can be acknowledged and supported without making clients responsible for meeting them.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-013","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-014","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Refusal and boundaries","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client declines non-urgent assistance with dressing and says, “I want to do it myself.” There is no immediate safety emergency. Which response best combines reflection and person-centred care?","choices":["Ask what support, if any, the client wants, consider suitable alternatives within the care plan, and report/document relevant facts under workplace procedures.","Complete the task because efficiency protects everyone’s time.","Leave without clarifying and assume all future assistance is refused.","Tell the client that refusal proves they do not understand their needs."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Clarification supports choice without coercion. Relevant communication and documentation support continuity. This educational scenario does not establish a blanket legal rule or authorize independent changes outside the HCA’s role.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-014","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-015","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Self-awareness","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Following that refusal, the HCA thinks, “They are rejecting me personally.” Which reflective question is most useful?","choices":["Which defence mechanism proves the client dislikes me?","How can I persuade them to show respect?","What evidence supports my interpretation, and how might my need to feel useful be shaping it?","Which colleague is best at making this client comply?"],"answer":[2],"explain":"Separating observed refusal from personal interpretation reveals assumptions and emotional needs. A refusal of assistance does not establish rejection of the worker.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-015","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-016","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Objective documentation","difficulty":2,"prompt":"At 08:20 a client says, “No shower now; I’m cold.” The HCA offers to return later, the client agrees, and the HCA informs the supervising nurse at 08:25. Which entry best distinguishes observation from judgment?","choices":["Client manipulative and noncompliant again; nurse aware.","08:20: Client stated, “No shower now; I’m cold.” Offered to return later; client agreed. Supervising nurse informed at 08:25.","Client refused hygiene because of an attention-seeking personality.","Client would not cooperate but eventually became reasonable."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The entry uses a relevant quote, records the response and notification, and avoids inferred motives or character labels. Actual charting must also follow workplace requirements.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-016","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-017","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Confidentiality","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA wants to reflect with a colleague on a distressing interaction. Which approach best protects confidentiality while preserving learning?","choices":["Post an unnamed story online containing enough unique details to identify the client.","Copy the full care record into a personal journal.","Use an authorized private setting and share only information needed for the professional discussion, following policy.","Discuss identifiable details in a public café because the purpose is educational."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Purpose alone does not make disclosure appropriate. A private authorized discussion and limited relevant detail support confidentiality. Removing a name may not sufficiently de-identify a distinctive story.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-017","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-018","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Mature defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"After being criticized, an HCA recognizes an impulse to throw something. Later, they channel that aggressive energy into a vigorous game of squash. What mechanism is most specifically illustrated?","choices":["Projection.","Displacement.","Sublimation.","Repression."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Sublimation channels an unacceptable impulse into a personally and socially acceptable activity. Displacement redirects emotion unhealthily toward another target.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-018","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-019","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Mature defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA recognizes sadness during a busy shift, deliberately postpones processing it until a planned debrief, and attends that debrief. Which mechanism fits best?","choices":["Suppression.","Denial.","Reaction formation.","Repression."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Suppression involves temporarily setting a thought or feeling aside and returning to it. Awareness and intentional postponement distinguish it from unconscious repression or refusal to accept reality.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-019","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-020","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Mature defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Before a potentially difficult family meeting, an HCA considers likely stressors and prepares appropriate ways to respond. What is the most specific mature mechanism?","choices":["Fantasy.","Anticipation.","Projection.","Isolation of affect."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Anticipation involves preparing for expected future difficulty. It differs from withdrawing into an imaginary resolution or using detail to avoid feeling.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-020","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-021","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Mature defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA copes with personal distress by participating in a suitable community volunteer activity, with concern for others and no expectation of praise. Which mature mechanism is most directly illustrated?","choices":["Displacement.","Reaction formation.","Denial.","Altruism."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Altruism involves selfless concern for others. An emotional benefit to the helper does not by itself make the act approval-seeking. The scenario explicitly excludes an expectation of praise.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-021","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-022","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Mature defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which use of humour best fits the chapter’s mature response?","choices":["Laughing about a client’s private difficulty with unrelated staff.","Joking that a serious concern is imaginary.","Making a joke that acknowledges a shared difficulty and eases tension without dismissing pain.","Using sarcasm to stop a client from expressing fear."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Mature humour permits expression and reduces tension while recognizing seriousness. Dismissal, humiliation, and inappropriate disclosure are not justified by calling them humour.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-022","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-023","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Other defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"After being reprimanded by a supervisor, a worker goes home and shouts at a roommate over a minor inconvenience. Which mechanism is most directly illustrated?","choices":["Suppression.","Anticipation.","Displacement.","Sublimation."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The strong feelings are redirected unhealthily from the original person to another. The aggression has not been transformed into an acceptable outlet.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-023","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-024","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Other defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker is angry with a colleague but does not acknowledge it. Instead, the worker becomes convinced that the colleague is angry with them. What mechanism fits this pattern?","choices":["Displacement.","Projection.","Repression.","Altruism."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Projection attributes one’s unacceptable feeling to another person. Displacement instead changes the target of the expressed feeling. These are teaching patterns, not diagnoses from a single observation.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-024","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-025","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Other defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which feature most clearly distinguishes repression from suppression in this chapter?","choices":["Repression is an unconscious removal from awareness; suppression is a temporary decision to put something aside.","Repression concerns future events; suppression concerns the past.","Repression uses written reflection; suppression does not.","Repression always involves directing anger at another person."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The defining distinction is awareness and temporary postponement versus unconscious exclusion. Directing anger toward another target describes displacement.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-025","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-026","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Other defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A person’s conscious feelings toward someone are the opposite of their underlying unconscious wishes. Which mechanism best matches that definition?","choices":["Reaction formation.","Anticipation.","Sublimation.","Fantasy."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Reaction formation involves consciously experiencing the opposite of underlying unconscious wishes or emotions. Merely behaving politely while knowingly angry would not establish it.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-026","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-027","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Other defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"During a painful personal event, a person focuses excessively on schedules, terminology, and technical detail to avoid experiencing emotion. Which response best fits the chapter’s terminology?","choices":["Humour.","Projection.","Altruism.","Isolation of affect / intellectualisation."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The chapter groups isolation of affect with intellectualisation and describes using conscious detail to avoid uncomfortable emotion. Accurate practical planning alone would not establish this mechanism.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-027","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-028","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Other defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A person temporarily refuses to believe distressing news but later begins to acknowledge it. What conclusion is best supported?","choices":["Any denial establishes a psychiatric disorder.","Denial can temporarily help functioning; persistence and consequences matter when considering its effects.","Acknowledging reality later proves denial did not occur.","Denial and suppression are interchangeable because both are deliberate."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The chapter describes short-term denial as potentially helpful in grief and warns about over-reliance. Its category labels are not sufficient grounds for diagnosing a person.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-028","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-029","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Boundaries","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA feels angry after being insulted. Which response best combines accountability with appropriate boundaries?","choices":["Assume that taking responsibility for a response makes the insult acceptable.","Recognize the feeling, choose a safe response, set a respectful limit, and obtain appropriate help if safety requires it.","Treat anger as proof of professional failure.","Remain alone in an escalating situation to demonstrate self-control."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Self-awareness concerns managing one’s response, not accepting abuse or ignoring safety. The instructor’s example supports a respectful limit and appropriate support rather than retaliation.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-029","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-030","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Accountability","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A colleague says, “Reflection means recognizing that clients cannot be harmed emotionally by how we speak.” Which correction is best?","choices":["A reflective worker can predict and control every client emotion.","The client’s interpretation is irrelevant to reflection.","Only intentions matter if the worker remains calm.","Responsibility for managing our responses does not remove our duty to examine how our conduct affects others."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The course emphasis on personal emotional responsibility must not erase the impact of communication. Reflection examines both intentions and effects without claiming total control of another person’s feelings.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-030","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-031","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Self-awareness","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA notices feeling unusually impatient only when a client resembles someone from a past conflict. Which next step best reflects self-awareness?","choices":["Explore the association and its effect on interpretation, then plan how to keep care responsive to this client.","Ask the client to avoid reminding the HCA of that person.","Stop examining the reaction because its cause is outside work.","Assume the client has the same intentions as the person from the past."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Past experience can shape present reactions. Recognizing that influence allows the HCA to question assumptions rather than transfer an old relationship onto the current client.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-031","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-032","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Feedback","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A colleague offers feedback that conflicts with an HCA’s self-image. Which response most supports reflective learning?","choices":["Reject it unless the colleague has the same personality.","Ask whether other staff like the HCA before discussing examples.","Accept every interpretation as a proven fact.","Compare specific examples with personal observations, examine defensiveness, and identify useful changes."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Feedback is information to examine honestly, not automatically accept or reject. Specific evidence supports learning about strengths and areas for improvement.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-032","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-033","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Models","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Two HCAs use different reflective models and both identify justified changes that improve communication. What is the strongest conclusion?","choices":["Only one model can be legitimate.","A model can be selected for fit with the individual’s thinking and learning needs while retaining depth and application.","A model is useful only when it prevents uncomfortable feelings.","Models remove the need to consider evidence and ethics."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The chapter supports selecting a suitable model. Its purpose is to guide thoughtful exploration, not replace judgment, evidence, or ethical consideration.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-033","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-034","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Critical reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker interprets a client’s limited eye contact as disrespect. What is the most critically reflective response?","choices":["Use the interpretation as an objective observation.","Explore personal expectations and relevant context, and clarify meaning without assuming a cultural explanation.","Assume limited eye contact always has the same cultural meaning.","Avoid communication because clarification could be uncomfortable."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Critical self-awareness challenges the worker’s interpretation. Context and the individual’s perspective should be explored; a cultural stereotype would simply replace one unsupported assumption with another.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-034","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-035","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Scope and support","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker’s reflection identifies a situation beyond their knowledge and role. What is the best next step?","choices":["Conclude that reflection has failed because outside help is needed.","Tell the client the worker’s personal history to establish credibility.","Invent a solution so the reflective cycle can be completed independently.","Seek appropriate supervision or referral and use the learning to guide future communication within the role."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The chapter recognizes that no professional has every answer and that appropriate support or referral may be needed. Insight into limitations is a useful reflective outcome.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-035","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-036","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Reflective practice","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which evidence most strongly demonstrates reflective practice over time?","choices":["The worker can recite all model labels.","The worker produces increasingly long descriptions.","The worker identifies a recurring trigger, applies a justified change, and reviews its effects across later encounters.","The worker never experiences discomfort again."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Reflective practice involves using learning to change or reinforce behaviour over time. Label recall and volume of writing do not establish application, and discomfort can still occur.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-036","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-037","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Gender preference","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client requests a female caregiver for intimate care. The assigned male HCA feels personally rejected. Which response best integrates self-awareness and client-centred care?","choices":["Promise that only female workers will attend from now on.","Explain that professional competence should make any assigned worker acceptable.","Acknowledge the preference, notify the supervisor, and explore available options within staffing and policy.","Record that the client discriminates against all male staff."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Acknowledge the stated preference without treating it as a judgment of personal worth. Staffing arrangements require appropriate coordination; the HCA should neither guarantee an unavailable option nor assign an unsupported motive.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-037","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-038","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Refusal","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client declines a shower. Which opening question most effectively tests the HCA’s assumption that the client is being difficult?","choices":["Who taught you to distrust caregivers?","Why are you refusing to cooperate again?","Would you prefer a different time, or is something about the shower uncomfortable?","You understand that I need to finish, don’t you?"],"answer":[2],"explain":"The question opens discussion of timing and discomfort without presuming a motive. Leading, accusatory, and speculative questions undermine clarification.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-038","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-039","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Refusal","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client says shower water felt cold yesterday. Which response best distinguishes a useful hypothesis from an established conclusion?","choices":["Cold water explains all future refusals by this client.","A refusal cannot have more than one contributing factor.","Yesterday’s experience may contribute to today’s refusal; ask what would help before assuming that is the only reason.","The client has proved that staff intentionally caused discomfort."],"answer":[2],"explain":"A past experience can inform exploration without proving an exclusive cause. Reflection considers multiple factors and checks interpretations.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-039","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-040","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Person-centred planning","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA learns that a client declines morning care because the timing conflicts with a valued routine. What is the most useful reflective learning?","choices":["A changed schedule guarantees no further refusal.","Client routines should be ignored when care is scheduled.","The HCA’s assumption that one schedule suits everyone needs reconsideration.","The refusal proves the original care plan was negligent."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The specific learning concerns an assumption about timing and individual needs. It supports coordinated review without a guaranteed outcome or unsupported legal conclusion.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-040","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-041","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Autonomy and scope","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client with a disability declines recommended care. A coworker says, “Their disability means we can decide for them.” Which response is best supported?","choices":["An HCA should independently determine capacity before speaking with the team.","Disability alone does not establish incapacity; report relevant concerns for appropriate assessment and follow authorized processes.","Family preference automatically resolves the issue.","Every refusal proves decision-making capacity."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The brief explicitly distinguishes disability from incapacity. Concerns about decision-making require appropriate professionals and processes; neither capacity nor incapacity should be inferred solely from refusal.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-041","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-042","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Autonomy and risk","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client chooses food inconsistent with dietary advice. No immediate emergency is present. Which response best preserves role boundaries?","choices":["Ask another client to persuade them.","Ignore the choice because autonomy removes every reporting duty.","Confiscate the food because advice is equivalent to an enforceable order.","Clarify the situation, communicate relevant concerns within scope, and follow the care plan and reporting process without assuming authority to confiscate."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Respect for choice coexists with appropriate communication, reporting, and the care plan. The scenario does not grant authority to confiscate or justify involving an unrelated client.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-042","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-043","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Wound-care refusal","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client declines wound care and says, “It hurt last time.” Which HCA response best supports informed team follow-up?","choices":["Perform the care without discussing refusal to prevent infection.","Acknowledge the reported pain, report the refusal and concern promptly through the appropriate channel, and follow the plan within scope.","Diagnose osteomyelitis from the refusal.","Tell the client that sepsis is inevitable if they refuse once."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The concern warrants communication and appropriate escalation, including the pain barrier. Possible complications are not inevitable outcomes, and refusal alone does not diagnose infection.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-043","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-044","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Professional development","difficulty":2,"prompt":"During reflection on wound-care refusal, an HCA realizes their own knowledge is limited. Which goal most closely follows the instructor’s professional-development example?","choices":["Memorize complications to frighten clients into agreement.","Avoid reporting until advanced expertise is achieved.","Learn relevant information through approved education and supervision so observations and concerns can be communicated more effectively.","Independently take over treatment decisions."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The instructor used reflection to identify wound-care learning needs. Education should improve practice within role, not become a basis for independent treatment decisions or coercion.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-044","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-045","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Emotional regulation","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA feels anxious about possible complications after a refusal. Which reflection best separates the worker’s emotion from the client’s decision?","choices":["Feeling anxious means the client made the wrong decision.","If I remain calm, there is no need to report relevant concerns.","My anxiety authorizes me to override the client.","I should identify my anxiety, communicate relevant concerns appropriately, and avoid using fear to pressure the client."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Managing anxiety supports clear communication. It neither establishes the right decision nor replaces reporting or authorized procedures.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-045","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-046","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Documentation and scope","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client refuses an intervention after an explanation. Which documentation approach is most defensible?","choices":["Omit the refusal if the client appeared calm.","Document only the worker’s belief that the client understood.","Record the observed refusal, relevant client words, what was offered or done, and notifications without claiming an assessment outside the HCA’s role.","Write “fully informed refusal” solely because an HCA gave an explanation."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Objective recording captures the encounter without asserting an independent capacity or informed-consent assessment that was not established. Calmness does not make relevant facts unimportant.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-046","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-047","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Home-care boundaries","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A home-care client calls the HCA “the only person who understands me” and asks for private visits outside work. The HCA feels needed. What is the most reflective response?","choices":["Recognize the appeal to the HCA’s emotional needs and seek guidance on maintaining the professional relationship and appropriate supports.","Promise secrecy to preserve trust.","Tell the client that needing connection is inappropriate.","Accept because the client initiated the request."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Familiarity and feeling needed can blur boundaries. Acknowledge the need for connection and seek appropriate support without converting the relationship into a private arrangement.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-047","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-048","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Emotional needs","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA gives extra attention to a client who offers praise and less attention to a quieter client with comparable planned needs. What is the central reflective concern?","choices":["The worker should ask the quieter client to express more gratitude.","The worker may be allowing a personal need for approval to shape care priorities.","Praise necessarily invalidates every caring act.","Quiet clients prefer less care by definition."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The pattern suggests a possible influence of approval-seeking on care. The worker should examine and correct that influence rather than make clients supply praise.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-048","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-049","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Self-worth","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker reflects, “I want to be useful, and that is why I become upset when a client completes a task independently.” What future response best follows?","choices":["Make assistance harder to decline.","Ask the client to accept unnecessary help to protect the worker’s confidence.","Support the client’s appropriate independence and find healthy ways to understand personal worth beyond being needed.","Treat independence as evidence that the care relationship has failed."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The worker’s sense of usefulness should not displace client goals. Reflection can reveal the need and support a different understanding of personal value.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-049","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-050","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Reflexivity","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client begins shouting. The HCA notices an urge to match their volume. Which response most directly demonstrates reflexive practice?","choices":["Recognize how the urge could shape the interaction and adjust tone in the moment while following safety procedures.","After the shift, list every insult.","Assume a quieter voice guarantees safety.","Explain that the client is responsible for making the HCA angry."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Reflexivity includes examining personal influence while interacting. A calmer response can help but does not guarantee safety or replace procedures.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-050","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-051","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Strategies versus defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA chooses a neutral tone before entering a potentially tense encounter. Which interpretation is most precise?","choices":["It is displacement because the HCA is changing emotional expression.","A neutral tone is automatically repression.","It can be a deliberate communication strategy; the action alone does not establish an unconscious defence mechanism.","It proves the HCA feels no anxiety."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The brief distinguishes conscious strategies from automatic assignment of defence labels. An observed tone alone does not establish the internal process.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-051","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-052","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Home-visit safety","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A home-care worker keeps a phone and keys accessible in accordance with the safety plan. What does this action most clearly demonstrate?","choices":["Projection.","Reaction formation.","Denial of danger.","A practical safety precaution."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The action is a deliberate precaution. It should not be labelled an unconscious defence mechanism merely because it helps the worker feel prepared.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-052","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-053","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Safety","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A home visit becomes unsafe. Which response best combines professional boundaries and reflection?","choices":["Retaliate to demonstrate authority.","Remain until the task is complete regardless of danger.","Follow workplace procedures for obtaining help and safely withdrawing, report the situation, and later examine relevant warning signs.","Write a reflection instead of obtaining help."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Immediate safety procedures take priority; reflection can follow to support learning. It does not replace assistance or authorize retaliation.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-053","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-054","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Source fidelity and safety","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An instructor describes receiving two weeks of mentorship before independent home visits. What is the best interpretation for future practice?","choices":["After two weeks, no visit can be unsafe.","Every employer must use exactly two weeks.","It is an example of supported preparation; actual orientation and supervision requirements depend on the workplace and role.","Mentorship replaces reading the care plan."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The brief identifies a personal example, not a universal duration or safety guarantee. Preparation remains linked to actual requirements.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-054","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-055","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Advocacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA observes a recurring difficulty that the visiting clinician has not seen. Which communication best supports advocacy?","choices":["On three visits, I observed this specific pattern; here are the client’s words and the assistance needed. Could the team review it?","I know the diagnosis, so the plan must change immediately.","The clinician never understands this client.","I will wait because only clinicians’ observations matter."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Concrete, relevant observations can add information the team lacks. Confident reporting supports advocacy without disparagement or independent diagnosis.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-055","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-056","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Care plan","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A care-plan instruction is unclear before a task begins. Which response best demonstrates accountable use of reflection?","choices":["Proceed and use reflection later as justification.","Guess based on what another client usually needs.","Clarify through the appropriate supervisor or authorized source before proceeding beyond what is understood and permitted.","Assume the most recent visitor knows the plan."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The instructor emphasizes reading the plan and using appropriate channels. Reflection should reveal a need for clarification, not excuse guessing.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-056","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-057","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Advocacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA reports a concern accurately but feels dismissed. Which next step best preserves advocacy and role boundaries?","choices":["Post the concern online to force a response.","Stop reporting future observations.","Change the care plan independently.","Use the appropriate escalation route, communicate the relevant observations clearly, and document according to policy."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Appropriate escalation maintains communication and accountability. It does not require independent treatment changes or public disclosure.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-057","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-058","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Privacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker considers making a video of a client’s behaviour on a personal phone to prove a concern. No authorization exists. What is the strongest response?","choices":["Record as long as the client’s face is excluded.","Record if the worker’s intention is helpful.","Ask another client to film it instead.","Use objective observations and approved reporting routes; do not make the unauthorized recording."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The brief explicitly warns against unauthorized recordings. Good intentions or excluding a face do not by themselves authorize recording or protect confidentiality.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-058","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-059","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Privacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A visitor begins reading a care plan left open on a table. Which response best supports confidentiality?","choices":["Read the rest aloud so no one misunderstands it.","Assume any visitor has permission.","Secure the information and follow workplace procedures for addressing the access, without disclosing further details unnecessarily.","Remove the client’s name but leave every other detail visible."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Access should not be inferred from visitor status. Securing the plan and following the relevant procedure protects information without inventing authority.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-059","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-060","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Reflective records","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA removes a client’s name from a personal journal but leaves the facility, exact room, date, and a rare event. Which conclusion is best?","choices":["The entry is anonymous because there is no name.","The rare event makes the entry exempt from confidentiality.","A journal can contain any details if it is handwritten.","The remaining combination may identify the client; remove identifying client/facility details and use approved reflective practices."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Identifiability depends on the combination of details. The brief specifically cautions against identifying client and facility information in personal reflections.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-060","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-061","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Documentation","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which sentence belongs most appropriately in personal reflective analysis rather than being treated as an objective clinical fact?","choices":["Client turned away when assistance was offered.","Supervising nurse notified at 09:15.","I wonder whether my fear of criticism made me interrupt.","Client stated, “I want to wait.”"],"answer":[2],"explain":"The sentence is an introspective hypothesis about the worker. The others describe statements, actions, or notification. Personal analysis and objective care documentation serve different purposes.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-061","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-062","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Treatment preferences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client expresses a treatment preference that appears inconsistent with the current documented orders. What should the HCA do?","choices":["Promptly report the statement through the appropriate channel and follow current authorized orders, care plans, and applicable emergency procedures.","Choose the order that best matches the HCA’s personal values.","Treat the statement as an independently completed order change.","Ignore the statement because orders cannot be reviewed."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Preferences should be communicated for appropriate review. An HCA should not assume the statement itself independently revises an order or disregard the expressed concern.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-062","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-063","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Communication between settings","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA notices that transfer information and the current care plan appear inconsistent. What is the best response?","choices":["Ask a visitor to choose which instructions apply.","Report and seek clarification through authorized channels, following applicable procedures for the current situation.","Delete the conflicting instruction from the record.","Select whichever document has the clearest handwriting."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Possible discrepancies need authorized clarification. The HCA should not resolve them by arbitrary selection, visitor preference, or unilateral editing.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-063","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-064","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Treatment orders","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A family member says, “You heard the client’s new wishes, so you can change the treatment order yourself.” Which reply best reflects role boundaries?","choices":["Verbal preferences are never relevant.","Only family wishes can change an order.","I can report what was expressed and seek the appropriate review; I cannot independently change the order.","Any worker may change an order if they document it."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The appropriate contribution is reporting and facilitating review within role. The other replies create unsupported absolutes or authority.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-064","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-065","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"End-of-life scope","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA encounters an unresponsive client in circumstances they did not expect. Which response is best?","choices":["Delay action until a reflective journal entry is complete.","Independently pronounce death before reporting.","Follow the applicable emergency/reporting procedures, care plan, and authorized directions; do not independently pronounce death.","Assume every unexpected death requires an autopsy before taking any action."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The brief emphasizes emergency/reporting procedures and the limit on independent pronouncement. It does not establish a universal autopsy requirement or justify delay.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-065","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-066","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"End-of-life scope","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An expected-death care plan is in place. Which statement best describes the HCA’s responsibility?","choices":["Decide privately whether the family needs notification.","Expected death eliminates all reporting requirements.","Use the same assumptions for every client regardless of their plan.","Follow the applicable plan and procedures and communicate observations; expected circumstances do not confer authority to pronounce death independently."],"answer":[3],"explain":"An expected situation still requires role-appropriate actions and reporting. The plan matters, and independent pronouncement is outside the role described.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-066","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-067","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Accountability","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A coworker says, “If I follow the plan and chart everything, I cannot be held accountable for negligence.” What is the strongest correction?","choices":["Accurate records replace appropriate care.","Documentation makes all actions legally safe.","Following the plan, reporting, and accurate documentation matter, but they do not guarantee immunity from liability.","Accountability applies only when nothing is written."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The source brief explicitly rejects a guarantee of immunity. The quiz does not determine liability in any particular case.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-067","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-068","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Grief and reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA wishes to reflect on a death that felt more distressing than expected. What is the most useful focus?","choices":["Whether feeling sadness proves the HCA is unfit.","A detailed account shared with unrelated friends for verification.","A diagnosis of every grieving family member.","Personal triggers, expectations, professional actions, support needs, and learning, with confidentiality maintained."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Reflection can support emotional processing and professional learning without equating sadness with failure or disclosing client information inappropriately.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-068","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-069","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Suppression","difficulty":2,"prompt":"While supporting a grieving family, an HCA recognizes distress and temporarily sets it aside to complete assigned responsibilities, intending to process it with appropriate support later. Which additional detail most strengthens the interpretation of suppression?","choices":["The HCA later acknowledges and works through the distress.","The HCA insists nothing distressing occurred.","The HCA redirects anger toward another client.","The HCA cannot consciously recognize the distress at any point."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Returning to a recognized feeling supports temporary suppression rather than denial, unconscious repression, or displacement.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-069","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-070","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Anticipation","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Before a stressful shift, a worker imagines likely communication challenges and rehearses suitable responses. Afterward, they review what happened. Which pairing is best?","choices":["Anticipation before the shift; reflection afterward.","Displacement before the shift; repression afterward.","Humour before the shift; fantasy afterward.","Projection before the shift; denial afterward."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Preparing for expected stress illustrates anticipation; examining an actual experience afterward illustrates reflection. Activities may work together without being identical.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-070","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-071","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Altruism","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker volunteers to help others but becomes resentful when nobody praises them. Which interpretation is most careful?","choices":["Every volunteer action is displacement.","Resentment proves the worker has no concern for others.","The expectation of praise warrants reflection on approval-seeking; do not assume the help was entirely selfless.","The action conclusively demonstrates selfless altruism."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The defining cue for altruism is selfless concern without seeking personal reward. Mixed motives require examination, not a categorical judgment that the person has no concern.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-071","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-072","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Humour","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client responds to a joke with silence and turns away. What is the best next response?","choices":["Explain that the client should appreciate humour.","Notice the response, check whether the remark was unwelcome, and adjust respectfully.","Conclude that all humour in healthcare is inappropriate.","Repeat the joke more loudly."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Appropriate humour depends on the person and situation. Observed discomfort calls for checking and adjusting, not defending intent or imposing a blanket ban.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-072","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-073","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Humour","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker uses sarcasm about a client’s fear and calls it a mature defence. What distinction matters most?","choices":["Humour is mature only when every observer laughs.","Any speech during stress is sublimation.","Mature humour relieves tension without dismissing pain; sarcasm that belittles fear may undermine care.","A mature label automatically makes the action beneficial."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The effect and context of humour matter. A defence label does not excuse insensitive conduct, and laughter is not the sole measure of appropriateness.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-073","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-074","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Sublimation","difficulty":2,"prompt":"After an insult, a worker states a respectful limit and obtains help. Later, they channel aggressive energy into a safe sport. Which action most specifically illustrates sublimation?","choices":["Obtaining help.","Channelling the aggressive impulse into the acceptable sporting activity.","Recognizing that an insult occurred.","Stating the limit."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Sublimation is the transformation of an unacceptable impulse into an acceptable outlet. Safety actions and awareness are appropriate but are not automatically the same mechanism.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-074","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-075","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Suppression and avoidance","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker says, “I postponed thinking about my distress,” but never returns to it and repeatedly avoids every reminder. Which interpretation is best?","choices":["Avoidance always improves communication.","The statement alone establishes effective suppression with resolution.","This proves unconscious repression beyond doubt.","The repeated avoidance warrants further reflection; temporary postponement has not clearly led to later processing."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The course definition emphasizes setting aside temporarily and returning later. The pattern raises concern without establishing a specific unconscious mechanism or diagnosis.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-075","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-076","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Mature distinctions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which pairing correctly matches two mature responses to their decisive cues?","choices":["Anticipation—refusing to accept past events; suppression—unconscious removal of memories.","Humour—denying seriousness; altruism—seeking praise.","Altruism—attributing anger to others; anticipation—redirecting anger to a housemate.","Suppression—temporary conscious postponement; sublimation—acceptable channelling of an unacceptable impulse."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The correct pair preserves awareness/postponement for suppression and constructive channelling for sublimation. Other pairs confuse projection, displacement, denial, and repression.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-076","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-077","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Displacement correction","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker says, “I noticed another person’s inadequacies, so that is displacement.” Which correction is most precise?","choices":["Noticing inadequacies alone defines projection.","Displacement means any critical thought.","Displacement redirects strong feelings toward a substitute target; noticing shortcomings alone establishes neither displacement nor projection.","Projection and displacement are synonyms."],"answer":[2],"explain":"This corrects the instructor’s false statement without replacing it with another misdefinition. Projection requires attributing one’s own unacceptable feelings or characteristics to another.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-077","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-078","chapter":6,"mode":"defences","topic":"Other mechanisms","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker avoids a real-life problem by repeatedly imagining a satisfying world in which the problem does not exist, without insisting that the imagined world is real. Which chapter response fits best?","choices":["Anticipation.","Fantasy.","Altruism.","Suppression."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The chapter describes fantasy as an imagined world meeting needs not met by reality. Anticipation prepares for actual future difficulty rather than substituting an imagined world.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-078","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-079","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Critical reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA receives praise after an interaction in which the client appeared rushed. What is the most critically reflective response?","choices":["Assume a rushed appearance establishes the client’s feelings.","Consider the feedback alongside the client’s responses, intentions, and other evidence before deciding what to retain or change.","Discard all positive feedback.","Praise proves the care was fully person-centred."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Reflection uses more than one perspective while separating observations from inferred feelings. Positive feedback can be useful without making further examination unnecessary.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-079","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-080","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Self, Person, environment","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA’s reflection identifies communication style, personality, beliefs, and emotional responses. What additional dimension prevents the account from becoming exclusively self-focused?","choices":["Assume everyone shares the HCA’s goals.","Consider the client’s perspective and relevant environmental and relational factors.","Replace every observation with a personality label.","Ignore the client because reflection concerns the self."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Self-awareness is central, but the chapter also examines the Person and environment, expectations, and effects of the interaction.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-080","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-081","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"What Why How","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker asks, “What happened? Why did I react this way? How can I respond more constructively?” What makes these questions useful?","choices":["They connect description, understanding, and future response rather than stopping at blame.","They guarantee a single correct solution for every client.","They prove that the other person’s conduct is acceptable.","They remove the need for evidence."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The instructor’s What/Why/How emphasis supports inquiry into events, causes, and action. These prompts do not guarantee a universal formula or excuse harmful conduct.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-081","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-082","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Value and needs","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA says, “My worth depends on whether clients need me today.” Which reflective challenge most closely addresses that belief?","choices":["Examine how worth, importance, and usefulness are being understood and whether self-respect has become dependent on client approval or dependence.","Ask clients to validate the HCA at each visit.","Increase client dependence to restore worth.","Assume the belief must be true because it feels powerful."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The brief highlights value and the chapter links individual worth to self-understanding and self-respect. Reflection can challenge reliance on clients to validate worth.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-082","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-083","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Journaling","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A reflective journal shows that the HCA becomes abrupt late in noisy shifts. What is the strongest next inference?","choices":["The worker’s personality cannot change.","The pattern should be removed from the journal because it is uncomfortable.","The pattern suggests factors to explore and address, while considering other influences and testing a practical change.","Noise conclusively causes every abrupt response."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Patterns generate useful hypotheses, not proof of a sole cause. Journaling supports development when linked to analysis and action.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-083","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-084","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Reflective habit","difficulty":2,"prompt":"After months of regular reflection, an HCA completes a thoughtful review more quickly. What conclusion best fits the chapter?","choices":["Practice may make reflection more efficient, provided depth and learning remain.","Faster reflection must be superficial.","Reflection is no longer needed.","Only lengthy writing counts as reflection."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The chapter notes that time may decrease with practice. Neither speed nor length alone establishes quality.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-084","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-085","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Positive reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA chooses to reflect only on encounters that felt embarrassing. What important learning opportunity is being missed?","choices":["Reflection on strengths prevents improvement.","Successful encounters require no examination of context.","Only embarrassing events reveal emotion.","Positive experiences can reveal effective actions worth repeating."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Reflection includes positive and negative experiences. Understanding successful communication supports deliberate repetition and identification of strengths.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-085","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-086","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Necessity and scope","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker realizes a repeated intervention adds no benefit and seems to irritate the client. What is the best reflective next step?","choices":["Continue without reporting because reflection is private.","Conclude that all care is unnecessary.","Examine its purpose and effects, then raise a justified concern for appropriate review rather than assuming authority to change the plan.","Stop any prescribed intervention independently."],"answer":[2],"explain":"Reflection may identify an unnecessary or unsuitable element, but resulting changes must respect role and authorized review.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-086","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-087","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Cycle application","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA has selected an event and described it. Which prompt most clearly advances the cycle toward exploring all reactions?","choices":["Which model name sounds most professional?","What proves the client was unreasonable?","How can I avoid identifying my own response?","What did I and the client say or show, what might those reactions mean, and how can I check my interpretation?"],"answer":[3],"explain":"Exploration includes self and Person and distinguishes observed responses from inferred meaning. It is broader than proving blame or naming a model.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-087","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-088","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Circular process","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker says the seven-step cycle is complete forever once a plan is applied. Which correction is best?","choices":["The cycle stops at goal setting.","Application generates further experience to reflect on, so learning continues.","A plan must never be applied.","Only a new workplace allows the cycle to restart."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The instructor emphasizes continuous, circular operation. Action informs the next round of reflection and adjustment.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-088","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-089","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Cycle first step","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A student begins a seven-step account with choosing an event but omits any commitment to reflection. Which course element is missing before event selection?","choices":["Documenting a predetermined outcome.","Choosing and allocating time to reflect.","Choosing the final answer.","Deciding the client’s diagnosis."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The verified diagram begins with choosing and allocating time to reflect on healthcare practice, then choosing a challenging or interesting event.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-089","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-090","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Model distinction","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which statement best distinguishes the seven-step cycle from the supplied Johns model?","choices":["Johns begins with choosing time, while the cycle never involves action.","The cycle begins with choosing/allocating time and moves through goals, plans, and action; Johns supplies seven structured lines of examination of an experience.","They must have identical labels because each has seven parts.","Only Johns allows any learning."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Both guide reflection, but their structures differ. The chapter supplies Johns’s description, causes, background, aims, consequences/feelings, alternatives, and learning.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-090","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-091","chapter":6,"mode":"care","topic":"Depth versus checklist","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A student writes seven Johns headings and answers each with “the client was difficult.” What is the main problem?","choices":["Johns excludes any consideration of the client.","The structure has been used mechanically without examining evidence, personal contribution, context, or learning.","The correct number of headings guarantees depth.","Reflection should always avoid uncomfortable events."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Johns is intended to deepen understanding, not merely complete a format. Repeated judgment does not supply the analysis the headings invite.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-091","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-092","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Johns aims and effects","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA intended to reassure a client but spoke over them. The client withdrew. Which reflection best integrates intention and consequence?","choices":["Good intent means there was no communication problem.","Only the client’s reaction matters; the worker’s aim is irrelevant.","Withdrawal proves the client wanted no care.","Examine the reassuring aim alongside the interruption and withdrawal, and explore how another approach might have affected the encounter."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Both aims and consequences are relevant. Neither good intention nor an observed response alone gives a complete account of meaning.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-092","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-093","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Johns background","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA examines whether a previous conflict, fatigue, and corridor noise affected an encounter. Which Johns element is most directly addressed?","choices":["Alternative actions only.","The final action plan only.","Relevant background and environmental information.","Future learning only."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The focus is on significant circumstances relating to people and environment. Other elements may later use this information, but are not the explicit focus here.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-093","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-094","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Reflexivity and position","difficulty":2,"prompt":"During an interaction, an HCA notices that their professional authority may be discouraging disagreement and explicitly invites the client’s preference. What does this most clearly illustrate?","choices":["An objective diagnosis of the client’s motive.","Denial that the interaction has any power difference.","Reflexive attention to the practitioner’s position and influence.","Displacement of the client’s emotion."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The brief emphasizes reflexivity as examining assumptions, position, and influence. This can occur in the moment and guide an adjustment.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-094","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-095","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Non-verbal self-awareness","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker says, “I was calm, so my nonverbal communication could not have affected the client.” What is the best response?","choices":["Non-verbal cues do not belong in reflection.","Personal emotional state and others’ perceptions can differ; examine posture, timing, expression, and the client’s response.","A calm feeling proves the client perceived reassurance.","Only spoken words affect interactions."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Reflection includes nonverbal behaviour and its possible influence. The worker’s internal calm does not establish how the interaction was experienced.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-095","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-096","chapter":6,"mode":"reflection","topic":"Changing intentions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A family meeting plan assumes that everyone still shares the same goal, although a recent event may have changed one person’s expectations. What should be clarified?","choices":["How to obtain agreement before hearing concerns.","Whether emotions can be excluded from the discussion.","Each participant’s current intentions, concerns, and understanding rather than relying solely on the earlier agreement.","Which family member can be blamed for delaying the meeting."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The chapter emphasizes checking purposes and expectations initially and throughout interaction, especially when events and emotions may have changed them.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-096","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-097","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Outcomes","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA reviews a successful interaction and identifies one response that helped the client participate. What is the strongest conclusion?","choices":["Retain the helpful response while checking whether it fits later situations.","Change the response because reflection should produce a different action each time.","Repeat the entire interaction unchanged because a good outcome validates every step.","Stop examining successful interactions and concentrate reflection on mistakes."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Reflection can justify retaining effective practice. A successful overall outcome does not establish that every step was necessary or will suit every client.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-097","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-098","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Johns model","difficulty":2,"prompt":"In Johns’s model, an HCA identifies an alternative response but does not examine why it was not used. What would most deepen this part of the reflection?","choices":["Explore the factors that prevented the alternative and its possible consequences.","Add more factual detail without examining the choice.","Choose the alternative as mandatory for every later encounter.","Record the alternative only if its success can be proved."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Considering alternatives includes why they were not selected and what might have followed; the alternative is not a guaranteed solution.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-098","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-099","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Emotional needs","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA recognizes a need for appreciation that becomes stronger after difficult visits. Which response best maintains professional boundaries?","choices":["Seek appropriate personal or professional support and examine how the need affects care.","Explain the need to clients and ask them to provide reassurance after each visit.","Spend extra time with appreciative clients while keeping assigned tasks unchanged.","Avoid acknowledging the need so it cannot enter the care relationship."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Self-awareness supports managing personal needs without making clients responsible for meeting them.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-099","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-100","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA deliberately postpones thinking about distress until a planned debrief, then discusses it there. Which mechanism best fits?","choices":["Suppression","Repression","Denial","Displacement"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Suppression is a conscious temporary postponement. Repression is unconscious removal from awareness; the deliberate return to the experience matters.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-100","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-101","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"One reaction resembles projection during a stressful interaction. Which interpretation is most defensible?","choices":["It is a possible pattern to explore in context, not sufficient evidence of a psychiatric diagnosis.","The reaction establishes a psychiatric diagnosis if the worker recognizes it afterward.","A single instance proves the person uses projection in all relationships.","Projection can be identified only after a formal diagnosis has already been made."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A defence-mechanism concept may support reflection. One observed episode does not justify a psychiatric diagnosis or a universal claim about the person.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-101","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-102","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Confidentiality","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A colleague from another service asks for an identifiable client story to help with a class assignment. Which factor matters most before sharing?","choices":["Whether there is an authorized purpose, appropriate access and an approved private method.","Whether the colleague works in health care and promises not to name the client.","Whether the class is educational rather than recreational.","Whether the colleague already knows the client’s diagnosis."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A health-care job title or educational purpose does not automatically authorize disclosure.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-102","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-103","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Documentation","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which entry best preserves a client’s words as an observation rather than presenting an interpretation as fact?","choices":["Client stated, “I want to wait,” when assistance was offered.","Client was avoiding care and said they wanted to wait.","Client said they wanted to wait because they dislike assistance.","Client was probably anxious and therefore asked to wait."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A relevant direct quotation can be an objective observation when identified as the client’s words. Motives require evidence and should not be invented.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-103","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-104","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA feels upset while reviewing a difficult encounter. Which interpretation best supports learning?","choices":["Discomfort can be part of useful reflection; examine its meaning and obtain support as needed.","The distress shows the model is unsuitable and the reflection should be abandoned.","The reflection is effective only if discomfort has disappeared by its end.","Feeling upset establishes that the HCA caused the client’s difficulty."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Emotional discomfort does not by itself show failure or fault. Reflection examines reactions and uses appropriate support and learning.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-104","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-105","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Reflexivity","difficulty":2,"prompt":"During a conversation, an HCA notices that their position may make the client reluctant to disagree. Which response most clearly demonstrates reflexivity?","choices":["Examine that influence in the moment and invite the client’s perspective without assuming agreement.","Wait until the end of the shift to list what each person said.","Treat the client’s silence as evidence that professional authority was helpful.","Ask a colleague afterward whether the HCA appeared confident."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Reflexivity examines how the practitioner’s assumptions, position and influence shape an interaction, including while it occurs.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-105","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-106","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Cycle","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A student selects a significant encounter before arranging any space for reflection. Which step comes first in this course’s seven-step cycle?","choices":["Choose and allocate time to reflect.","Establish the goal for the next encounter.","Develop a strategy for a similar situation.","Describe the client’s reactions in detail."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The supplied seven-step cycle begins with choosing and allocating time, followed by selecting an experience and developing the reflection.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-106","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-107","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Models","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Two course models each contain seven parts. What is the strongest reason not to treat their headings as interchangeable?","choices":["The reflective cycle organizes a learning process; Johns’s model structures examination of an experience.","The same number of parts establishes the same sequence even if the headings differ.","Johns’s model excludes learning, whereas the cycle excludes analysis.","A model can be compared with another only when they contain different numbers of parts."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The number of parts does not establish identical wording, sequence or purpose. Follow the distinctions in the supplied models.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-107","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-108","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Anticipation","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Before a difficult meeting, an HCA considers likely tensions and prepares suitable responses. Which mechanism is most specifically illustrated?","choices":["Anticipation","Suppression","Fantasy","Sublimation"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Anticipation prepares for expected stressors. Suppression postpones current distress; fantasy avoids a real-life problem; sublimation channels an impulse into an acceptable activity.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-108","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-109","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"After being reprimanded, a worker shouts at a housemate rather than the supervisor. Which mechanism is more specific than sublimation?","choices":["Displacement","Projection","Suppression","Reaction formation"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Displacement redirects feelings toward a substitute target. Sublimation channels an impulse into a socially acceptable activity rather than aggression toward another person.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-109","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-110","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Suppression","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Which detail most clearly distinguishes suppression from repression?","choices":["The person consciously sets the thought aside temporarily and can return to it.","The person cannot consciously recognize the thought that was removed from awareness.","The person attributes the feeling to someone else.","The person insists the distressing event did not happen."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Suppression is a conscious temporary postponement; repression is unconscious. Projection and denial describe different patterns.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-110","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-111","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Humour","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A joke was intended to reduce tension, but the client becomes quiet and turns away. Which response best evaluates its appropriateness?","choices":["Check the client’s response and adjust; a helpful intention does not establish a helpful effect.","Explain the intention again and assume the silence means the client now understands.","Classify the joke as mature humour because it reduced the HCA’s anxiety.","Avoid reviewing the effect because the wording contained no direct insult."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The effect and context matter. Calling a response humour does not justify dismissing or belittling another person’s pain.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-111","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-112","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Defences","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA deliberately uses a neutral tone while following a safety plan. What can be concluded from that action alone?","choices":["It may be a conscious communication strategy; it does not establish an unconscious defence mechanism.","It demonstrates repression because the worker did not express the feeling.","It demonstrates projection because the client may perceive the tone differently.","It demonstrates displacement because the tone differs from the worker’s emotion."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A chosen tone is not by itself evidence of an unconscious mechanism. Context and the relevant psychological process must support the interpretation.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-112","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-113","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Preference","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client requests a caregiver of another gender for intimate care. What is the most appropriate documentation approach?","choices":["Record the stated preference and relevant response without inventing a hostile motive.","Record rejection of the assigned worker because a preference establishes personal hostility.","Omit the preference unless the client also explains their private history.","Record that the requested staffing arrangement has been guaranteed before it is confirmed."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A preference should not automatically be interpreted as hostility. Follow policy, explore feasible options and document relevant facts.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-113","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-114","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Capacity","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client with a disability declines recommended care. Which conclusion about decision-making is best supported?","choices":["Disability alone does not establish incapacity for that decision.","Refusing recommended care establishes incapacity when a disability is present.","Making a choice establishes capacity for every possible decision.","A family member’s concern authorizes the HCA to determine incapacity."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Do not infer incapacity from disability or diagnose it independently. Report relevant concerns for the appropriate authorized assessment process.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-114","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-115","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Refusal","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client refuses non-urgent care. Which response best combines respect for choice with continuity of care?","choices":["Respect the refusal, clarify concerns without coercion, and report relevant facts under policy.","Respect the refusal and omit it from handoff because reporting would undermine autonomy.","Report the refusal only after the client’s family has approved it.","Keep offering the same care until the client accepts, then record acceptance alone."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Respecting choice does not remove appropriate reporting, documentation or follow-up responsibilities.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-115","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-116","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Risk language","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client refuses one wound-care visit. Which statement best avoids turning a possible risk into a certainty?","choices":["Report the refusal and relevant concerns; one refusal does not make sepsis inevitable.","Warn that sepsis is inevitable so the client can make an informed choice.","Infer that no infection risk exists because only one visit was refused.","Delay reporting until a complication can be confirmed by the HCA."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The HCA reports observations and concerns without predicting an inevitable outcome or making a diagnosis.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-116","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-117","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Boundaries","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA feels anxious after a capable client refuses care. What does the anxiety authorize?","choices":["No independent override; manage the reaction and follow the reporting and care processes.","An override if the HCA believes the likely outcome would otherwise be worse.","An override after asking a coworker to witness the refusal.","An override when the family shares the HCA’s anxiety."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The worker’s emotion is relevant to reflection, not independent authority to override a client’s decision.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-117","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-118","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Confidentiality","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA plans a personal reflection on a difficult visit. Which revision best preserves learning while protecting confidentiality?","choices":["Focus on reactions and learning, remove identifying details, and follow approved reflective practices.","Retain the room and exact date but replace the name with initials.","Copy the full record because private learning is different from public disclosure.","Include a rare diagnosis so an unrelated reader can verify the account."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Personal reflection should avoid identifying client and facility details and follow approved methods.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-118","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-119","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Privacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A story omits a client’s name but includes the exact room, date and a rare event. Which privacy judgment is strongest?","choices":["The combination may still identify the client.","The story is anonymous because no single detail states the name.","The story is anonymous if the writer changes the client’s initials.","The story is anonymous unless the client complains."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Identifiability can arise from a combination of contextual details; name removal alone is insufficient.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-119","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-120","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Privacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA wants to film a client’s behaviour to support a report, but no authorization exists. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Use objective observations and approved reporting channels rather than make the unauthorized recording.","Record briefly and seek consent before showing it to the supervisor.","Record without the client’s face because that removes all identifying information.","Record on a personal phone and delete the clip after the team meeting."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A helpful purpose does not authorize recording. Use approved documentation and reporting methods.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-120","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-121","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Advocacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA observes a recurring change that a visiting clinician has not witnessed. What contribution is most useful?","choices":["Report specific observations, timing and relevant client statements through the care team.","Wait for the clinician to observe it so the report will be valid.","Offer a likely diagnosis before describing the observations.","Treat the absence of a clinician’s observation as evidence the pattern is unimportant."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Day-to-day observations can add valuable information without replacing clinical assessment or diagnosis.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-121","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-122","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Advocacy","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA reports a change but cannot explain its medical cause. Which response best supports advocacy?","choices":["Describe the observed change and seek appropriate review without waiting to diagnose it.","Choose the most likely diagnosis so the report will receive attention.","Ask a family member to supply a diagnosis before reporting.","Wait until the same change occurs again and the cause becomes clear."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Confident advocacy relies on relevant observations and appropriate escalation; a diagnosis is not a prerequisite for reporting.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-122","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-123","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Orders","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client expresses new treatment preferences that appear inconsistent with an existing order. What is the HCA’s role?","choices":["Report the preference for authorized review without independently revising the order.","Revise the order once a second worker confirms hearing the statement.","Treat the preference as irrelevant until the family asks for a change.","Ask the client to sign the HCA’s note so it becomes a replacement order."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The statement is important to report. Hearing it does not give an HCA independent authority to change an order; follow applicable care and emergency procedures.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-123","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-124","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"End of life","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An expected-death care plan is in place. Which responsibility remains with the HCA?","choices":["Follow the plan and reporting procedures without independently pronouncing death.","Pronounce death because the plan establishes that death was expected.","Notify only the family because clinical reporting is unnecessary.","Choose an emergency response based on personal experience rather than the plan."],"answer":[0],"explain":"An expected-death plan guides actions; it does not confer independent pronouncement authority on an HCA.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-124","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-125","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"End of life","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA encounters an unexpected death and is unsure of the required reporting process. Which assumption should guide the response?","choices":["Use the applicable emergency and reporting procedures rather than assume every case requires an autopsy.","Wait for an autopsy decision before contacting the designated professional.","Treat the absence of an expected-death plan as authority to pronounce death.","Assume that coroner attendance is mandatory before any other action."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Follow the applicable reporting and emergency procedures promptly. Do not infer universal requirements or independent authority from uncertainty.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-125","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-126","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Accountability","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker follows the care plan and writes accurate notes. What is the most defensible conclusion about accountability?","choices":["Those practices support accountable care but do not guarantee immunity from liability.","Accurate notes transfer responsibility for the worker’s actions to the supervisor.","Following the plan makes reporting changes optional.","Documentation protects an action even when the worker knew conditions had changed."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Plans, reporting and accurate documentation matter, but no one of them guarantees legal immunity.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-126","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-127","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Safety","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An instructor describes receiving two weeks of mentorship before independent home visits. How should a student apply that example?","choices":["Use it as an example of supported preparation and check the actual workplace requirements.","Treat two weeks as a universal minimum that satisfies every employer.","Assume independent practice is authorized after the same elapsed time.","Use the example instead of checking current supervision arrangements."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The instructor’s experience illustrates mentorship, not a universal duration or authorization rule.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-127","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-128","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Development","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Reflection identifies a need to learn a clinical skill outside an HCA’s current authorization. Which next step follows?","choices":["Seek approved learning and supervision while continuing to respect role and authorization limits.","Practise independently because identifying the learning need establishes accountability.","Complete an online course and assume the skill is added to the job description.","Ask the client to authorize practice so the learning can be applied immediately."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Learning and authorization are distinct. Reflection can identify a need without expanding the worker’s role.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-128","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-129","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Outcomes","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client’s care task ends successfully, although the HCA interrupted several questions. What should the reflection conclude?","choices":["Review the interruptions as well as the positive result before deciding what to retain.","Retain every action because the final outcome demonstrates necessity.","Classify the whole encounter as ineffective because one action needs improvement.","Review the interruptions only if the client later complains."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A good outcome does not establish that every action contributed to it. Reflection can identify strengths and changes within one encounter.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-129","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-130","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Needs","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker recognizes feeling less valuable when clients become more independent. Which response best addresses the personal need?","choices":["Examine that belief and seek healthy support while encouraging the client’s appropriate independence.","Offer additional help so the client continues to depend on the worker.","Ask the client to accept some assistance to preserve the relationship.","Avoid acknowledging the feeling because doing so would create a boundary problem."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Personal needs should be recognized and managed without making clients responsible for maintaining the worker’s worth.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-130","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-131","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Self-awareness","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA feels rejected when a client declines help. Which statement best separates emotion from evidence?","choices":["Feeling rejected is a personal response to examine, not proof of the client’s intention.","The feeling confirms that the refusal concerns the HCA rather than the task.","If the feeling persists, it becomes an objective observation.","A client’s later agreement would prove the earlier feeling was accurate."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A feeling may reveal something about the worker’s interpretation. It does not by itself establish another person’s motive.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-131","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-132","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Journaling","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An experienced HCA completes a thoughtful reflection more quickly than before. Which criterion best determines whether it remains useful?","choices":["It still examines evidence, reactions and learning and informs later practice.","It takes at least as long as the worker’s earliest written reflections.","It removes all uncertainty about the client’s motives.","It produces a longer account each time it is repeated."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Efficiency can improve with practice. Depth, honesty and application matter more than writing length or elapsed time alone.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-132","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-133","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Models","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A worker uses a reflective model successfully in one encounter. How should they use it in later situations?","choices":["Use its structure to examine the new context and evaluate a suitable response.","Repeat the earlier response because the model already established it as effective.","Treat the model as a rule that guarantees the same result.","Skip exploration when a later encounter resembles the earlier one."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A reflective model supports inquiry and learning; it does not supply one guaranteed response for all contexts.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-133","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-134","chapter":6,"mode":"truefalse","topic":"Boundaries","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client verbally abuses an HCA. Which response best combines self-management and a professional limit?","choices":["Regulate the reaction, state a respectful limit and obtain help under safety procedures.","Avoid any limit because taking responsibility for feelings requires accepting the behaviour.","Match the client’s volume to demonstrate confidence before seeking help.","Remain alone until the task ends so the client does not interpret withdrawal as rejection."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Responsibility for managing one’s reactions can coexist with respectful limits, safety precautions and appropriate help.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-134","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-135","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Integrated refusal","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client declines a non-urgent shower because they are exhausted. The HCA feels a need to be trusted and wants to keep persuading until the client agrees. Which plan best integrates reflection, client choice and continuity of care?","choices":["Recognize the need for approval, clarify the client’s preferences without coercion, explore appropriate options, and report/document the relevant facts under policy.","Respect the refusal by ending the discussion and assume there is no need to communicate relevant concerns to the team.","Offer reassurance, continue persuading until agreement, and record the eventual acceptance as evidence that the concern was resolved.","Accept the refusal as a rejection of the HCA, arrange a different worker, and omit the emotional reaction from further reflection."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The worker should examine approval-seeking without making the client meet that need. Respectful clarification, appropriate options, communication and objective documentation support person-centred care. Respecting choice does not eliminate relevant reporting responsibilities.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-135","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-136","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Critical reflection","difficulty":2,"prompt":"After a difficult conversation, an HCA writes, “The client was aggressive because they hate dependence. I stayed calm, so I did nothing to contribute.” Which revision most improves the reflection?","choices":["Delete all observations about the client and focus entirely on the HCA’s feelings without planning any future response.","Describe specific words/actions, treat the proposed motive as a hypothesis, examine the HCA’s contribution and context, and identify a future approach to evaluate.","Replace “aggressive” with “upset” and retain the conclusion that calmness proves the HCA had no influence.","Keep the motive as a fact but add the client’s age and diagnosis to explain the behaviour."],"answer":[1],"explain":"The original account combines a broad label, an unverified motive and an unsupported conclusion about the worker. Reflection separates observation from inference and examines personal contribution, context and future learning. Calmness does not prove that communication was helpful.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-136","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-137","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Johns synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A family meeting produces an agreed care plan, but a participant becomes silent after an interruption. Which sequence best represents a complete Johns-model examination?","choices":["Describe the event; explore causes and purpose; consider background; examine aims; consider consequences and feelings; examine alternatives and why they were not chosen; identify learning for future reactions.","Describe the agreement; identify who caused the silence; choose a more efficient meeting format; confirm that agreement proves success.","Allocate reflection time; select an event; describe it; explore reactions; establish goals; develop plans; act on them.","Describe the event; review background and aims; record the final agreement; stop because the practical goal was achieved."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Johns’s seven elements include causes, context, aims, consequences/feelings, alternatives and future learning. The time–event–goals–plans–action sequence is the other reflective cycle. Agreement alone does not establish that everyone felt heard.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-137","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-138","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Cycle synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA rushes morning care when anxious about appearing slow. Which plan most fully completes the seven-step reflective cycle?","choices":["Describe the rushing, select a strategy immediately, and assume improvement because the strategy was well intended.","Allocate time, identify anxiety, and repeat the reflection until anxiety disappears before establishing any goal.","Allocate time, select and describe an encounter, explore all reactions, establish an observable goal, develop feasible strategies, then apply and review them.","Set a general goal to be better, apply it without a strategy, and judge success only by whether the worker feels efficient."],"answer":[2],"explain":"The cycle begins with choosing/allocating time and selecting an event, then links description and exploration of reactions to goals, plans and action. Application produces further experience for reflection. Observable goals and feasible strategies make the learning reviewable.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-138","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-139","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Defence comparison","difficulty":2,"prompt":"Following criticism, a worker (1) shouts at a housemate, (2) channels aggressive energy into squash, (3) consciously postpones distress until a debrief and returns to it, and (4) attributes their own unacknowledged anger to the supervisor. Which sequence correctly identifies the four responses?","choices":["Sublimation; displacement; suppression; reaction formation.","Projection; displacement; repression; sublimation.","Displacement; anticipation; denial; projection.","Displacement; sublimation; suppression; projection."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Displacement redirects emotion unhealthily to another target; sublimation channels the impulse into an acceptable outlet; suppression temporarily postpones recognized distress; projection attributes one’s own unacknowledged feeling to another. These stipulated examples are not diagnoses.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-139","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-140","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Confidentiality synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA drafts a personal reflection containing a client’s name, room, rare condition and family details, intending to email it to a friend who formerly worked in healthcare. Which revision best protects confidentiality while preserving learning?","choices":["Send only a copy of the care record so the friend can independently assess the client before discussing the worker’s feelings.","Use an appropriate authorized support route and approved private method, limit identifying information, and focus reflection on the worker’s reactions and learning.","Remove the name but retain the room and rare condition, because former healthcare workers may receive any de-identified story.","Keep all details but ask the friend to promise secrecy before opening the personal email."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Professional support should use an authorized route and appropriate information handling. Removing a name may not prevent identification. Former employment, good intentions and promises of secrecy do not by themselves authorize disclosure.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-140","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-141","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Gender preference synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client requests a female worker for intimate care. The male HCA feels embarrassed and interprets this as incompetence. Which response best addresses both care and self-awareness?","choices":["Record hostility toward male staff and ask the team to avoid discussing the request with the client again.","Promise that only female workers will attend from now on, because acknowledging a preference requires guaranteeing it.","Acknowledge the preference, question the personal interpretation, seek supervisory guidance on available options, and document relevant facts and actions without inventing motives.","Explain professional competence until the client withdraws the request, then document that the misunderstanding was corrected."],"answer":[2],"explain":"A stated preference is not evidence of incompetence or hostility. Respectful acknowledgment and coordinated options support dignity; staffing arrangements should not be promised beyond authority. Documentation records what was actually said and done.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-141","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-142","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Autonomy synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client with a disability chooses food inconsistent with advice. A coworker proposes confiscating it because the client “cannot make good decisions.” No immediate emergency is described. Which response is best?","choices":["Let the coworker decide, because responsibility follows whoever first noticed the food.","Do not infer incapacity from disability; clarify the choice, consult the plan, communicate concerns within scope, and seek appropriate review without assuming authority to confiscate.","Assume refusal of advice proves capacity and omit reporting because any team involvement would be coercive.","Treat disability as enough evidence to override the choice, provided the food is returned later."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Disability alone does not establish incapacity, and refusal alone does not establish capacity. Respectful clarification, the care plan, role-appropriate reporting and appropriate professional assessment address the concern without unsupported authority.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-142","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]}]);
